<h1>2.2 Validation: ETag, Last-Modified and 304 Not Modified</h1>
<p class="lead">When a cached copy is stale, the browser does not download everything again. It asks "has this changed since my copy?", and if not, the server answers with a tiny 304 response.</p>

<div class="presenter" data-video="2.2" hidden></div>
<div class="story" data-story="2.2"></div>

<div class="rw-head"><h2>&#127757; In the real world</h2></div>
<p class="rw-sub">How the biggest companies on the internet meet the idea from this lesson.</p>
<div class="rw-grid">
    <div class="rw-card" style="--rw:#6e5494">
        <div class="rw-company"><span class="rw-logo">🐙</span>GitHub</div>
        <div class="rw-stat">304s are free<small>conditional requests save rate limit</small></div>
        <p class="rw-text">GitHub&#x27;s REST API returns an ETag on responses. If your client sends it back with If-None-Match and nothing changed, GitHub answers 304 Not Modified, and that request does not count against your rate limit. Polling tools save thousands of requests this way.</p>
        <div class="rw-lesson"><b>Lesson:</b> ETags turn &quot;download again&quot; into a tiny &quot;still the same&quot;.</div>
        <div class="rw-source">Source: GitHub REST API documentation, conditional requests</div>
    </div>
</div>



<details class="deep">
<summary>&#128214; Want to understand it deeply? Read the full explanation</summary>

<h2>Validators: a fingerprint for a response</h2>
<p>A <b>validator</b> is a small value that identifies one version of a response. The server sends it with the response, the browser stores it with its copy, and sends it back later to ask whether its copy is still current.</p>
<div class="grid grid-2">
    <div class="card">
        <b>ETag (entity tag)</b>
        <p class="muted">An identifier for this exact version of the content, for example a hash. Any change gives a new ETag.</p>
        <pre><code>Response: ETag: "c65a19797904665f"
Request:  If-None-Match: "c65a19797904665f"</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
        <b>Last-Modified</b>
        <p class="muted">The date and time the content last changed. Only precise to the <b>second</b>.</p>
        <pre><code>Response: Last-Modified: Thu, 01 Jan 2026 00:00:00 GMT
Request:  If-Modified-Since: Thu, 01 Jan 2026 00:00:00 GMT</code></pre>
    </div>
</div>

<h2>The conversation</h2>
<div class="flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>1. First request</b><small>No validator yet</small></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</div>
    <div class="flow-step miss"><b>200 OK + body</b><small>with <code>ETag</code> and <code>Last-Modified</code></small></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>2. Later request</b><small><code>If-None-Match: "etag"</code></small></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</div>
    <div class="flow-step hit"><b>304 Not Modified</b><small>No body. "Your copy is still good."</small></div>
</div>
<p>If the content changed, the ETag no longer matches and the server returns a normal <b>200</b> with the new body and the new ETag.</p>

<h2>Rules worth knowing</h2>
<div class="story mini" data-story="2.2-ifmatch"></div>
<ul>
    <li><b>ETag wins.</b> If a request has both <code>If-None-Match</code> and <code>If-Modified-Since</code>, the server must use the ETag and ignore the date.</li>
    <li><b>Strong vs weak ETags.</b> <code>"abc"</code> is strong: byte-for-byte identical. <code>W/"abc"</code> is weak: "means the same", for example the same data with different whitespace or compression.</li>
    <li><b>304 saves bandwidth, not always server work.</b> In this demo the server still reads the database to build the ETag, so a 304 is still slow (800 ms) but sends no body. Real systems make validation cheap by building the ETag from a version number or <code>UpdatedAt</code> column, or by caching it.</li>
    <li><b>ETags also protect writes.</b> <code>If-Match: "etag"</code> on a PUT means "only update if nobody changed it since I read it" (412 Precondition Failed otherwise). This is optimistic concurrency, built on the same idea.</li>
</ul>

</details>

