<h2>Experiments: do these in order</h2>
<ol class="steps">
    <li><b>Get a validator.</b> Click <em>Normal request</em>. You get <span class="status-200">200</span> with a body of a few hundred bytes. The ETag and Last-Modified are saved.</li>
    <li><b>Ask with the ETag.</b> Click <em>Send If-None-Match</em>. You get <span class="status-304">304</span> with a body of <b>0 bytes</b>.</li>
    <li><b>Ask with the date.</b> Click <em>Send If-Modified-Since</em>. Also 304, because the product has not changed since that date.</li>
    <li><b>Change the data.</b> Click <em>Change price in database</em>, then <em>Send If-None-Match</em> again. Now you get 200 with the new price and a <b>new</b> ETag, because the content changed. Send it once more: 304 again, using the new ETag.</li>
    <li><b>Think about scale.</b> A product list of 200 KB polled by 10,000 users every minute is 2 GB per minute. If the list rarely changes, 304s turn that into a few MB.</li>
</ol>

<h2>What you should take away</h2>
<div class="callout tip">
    <strong>Key points</strong>
    Validators let a stale copy be reused after a quick check. A 304 has no body, so it saves bandwidth and parsing. Prefer ETags, they are exact. Last-Modified is a simpler fallback with one-second precision. <code>no-cache</code> plus an ETag is a great default for API data that must always be current but is often unchanged.
</div>
<div class="callout danger">
    <strong>Common mistakes</strong>
    Generating the ETag from something that changes on every request (like a timestamp of when the response was built), so it never matches. Using a different ETag on each server behind a load balancer for the same content. Forgetting that computing the ETag can be as expensive as the full response if it needs the same database query.
</div>

